  "selected": [
    {
      "id": "eof-modes-and-pdo-index",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "methods/eof-modes-and-pdo-index.md",
      "sha256": "3544f2f552ecd0f41c41eb09edf7012f49ac8421900f2b571b8498d37f2726b1",
      "reason": "The question's whole computation is a leading-EOF PDO with area-weighted EOFs, a sign convention, grid/domain/record-dependent explained variance and an amplitude read from the regression pattern on the standardised index — this fragment fixes how those are defined and compared.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "Form anomalies by removing the climatological annual cycle AND the global-mean SST anomaly at each grid point, and weight cells by sqrt(cos(latitude)) before the EOF; the sign convention is positive index = negative SST anomalies in the central/western North Pacific, applied to both pattern and index.",
        "Amplitude = area-weighted RMS of the regression pattern (anomalies regressed on the standardised index), in °C per standard deviation — not the dimensionless EOF; compare patterns across sources with `analyze_correlation(weighting='area')`, since the domain reaches high latitudes."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "anomaly-baselines-and-climate-indices",
      "severity": "must",
      "path": "methods/anomaly-baselines-and-climate-indices.md",
      "sha256": "e0afb5c187aca1e075f9549153b1e896320bb0539544f66d3293fab4b4b77c92",
      "reason": "The question defines anomalies relative to a 1950-2014 monthly climatology, so the baseline rule and the requirement not to mix seasons/thereby the offset between series on different baselines govern the index construction and its comparison with the published series.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "Take each model's anomaly against its own 1950-2014 climatology of the same calendar month (this removes the seasonal cycle); forcing an observed climatology on a model index puts its mean-state bias into the index.",
        "The baseline is part of the answer: state the climatology/baseline used, and read an offset between the ERSSTv5-derived index and the published `pdo` (built on its own baseline) as a baseline difference, not an error."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "common-comparison-mask",
      "severity": "must",
      "path": "methods/common-comparison-mask.md",
      "sha256": "bbb98200f52b4a692ce097704b8c438179a795a44bec6b856c03cf31931af4ea",
      "reason": "Pattern correlations between each model's regression pattern and the ERSSTv5 pattern exist only over cells the two share, so the mask/regridding rule of this fragment constrains the reported spatial correlations and the model ranking.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "Do not mask the regression patterns to one shared mask: each mode was computed on its own source's cells; correlate each model pattern with the ERSSTv5 pattern over the cells the two share (`analyze_correlation` leaves out cells either one lacks).",
        "If a shared mask is built, `ocean_only=True` is right only when WOA23 is part of the comparison; here the reference is ERSSTv5, so use `ocean_only=False`."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "cmip6-tos-bulk-top-cell",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "concepts/cmip6-tos-bulk-top-cell.md",
      "sha256": "4acdeba0f49784d4435dbc4eed34ca9777254313daa951641759fa02df24cf64",
      "reason": "The CMIP6 variable being analysed is `tos`, and this fragment states it is top-cell bulk temperature rather than skin temperature, which must be reflected when comparing it with ERSSTv5 SST.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "`tos` is the bulk temperature of the top model cell (including under sea ice), not a skin value, and top-cell thickness differs (CESM2 0–10 m, GFDL-ESM4 0–2 m, MPI-ESM1-2-LR 0–12 m) — a definitional difference against ERSSTv5.",
        "Sea-ice-covered cells hold under-ice water near freezing and are not missing; make ocean masks consistent before differencing."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "serial-correlation-and-significance",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "methods/serial-correlation-and-significance.md",
      "sha256": "fe11bd008257a589f44e369faba5fa31ab3cf9ad99280e88c763ac45d2b0e917",
      "reason": "The requested lag-1-year autocorrelation and the Pearson correlations of indices are on strongly autocorrelated monthly series, so effective sample size and the limits of correlation significance must be stated with the numbers.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "Pattern (spatial) correlations have no effective-sample-size correction: quote the coefficient of each model-vs-ERSSTv5 pattern correlation, not a p-value.",
        "The ERSSTv5-derived index against the published PDO series compares two estimates of the same quantity: correlate as they are (`detrend=False`); their shared trend is part of the agreement, not a spurious link."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "omip-native-grid-vs-regridded",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "methods/omip-native-grid-vs-regridded.md",
      "sha256": "8ff046ffbadc5794fcd2f8fe93d3bb947c736dec650aa3a3cd6e2412b7da8e68",
      "reason": "The question prescribes bilinear regridding to a 1-degree grid for the EOF/regression patterns, and this fragment says regridding alters masks and conservation and should be used only for maps and pattern comparisons.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "Put all four sources, observations included, on the common 1° grid before the EOF and take explained variance and regression pattern there; compare explained variance only between fields on the same grid over the same period.",
        "The question prescribes bilinear regridding; the fragment holds 'conservative' is the right choice going to a coarser grid. Follow the question's bilinear and state that the regridded pattern correlation depends on the method."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "cmip6-grid-labels",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "datasets/cmip6-grid-labels.md",
      "sha256": "e349f9e305621b6c10b35cccd0490da521cc7e3ba08bfb53890479f81024d117",
      "reason": "The three models are not all on the same grid type (CESM2 and MPI-ESM1-2-LR are gn, GFDL-ESM4 is gr), which affects the masks and the meaning of the interpolation before the EOF and pattern correlation.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "GFDL-ESM4 `tos` is `gr`, already remapped (conserve_order1) to 1°, so its \"native\" numbers are numbers on the archive grid and are only approximately conservative.",
        "Resolution differs about 2.5× (CESM2 100 km, MPI 250 km), so features narrower than the coarse grid are not comparable in amplitude — state this with the amplitude comparison."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "monthly-means-are-time-averages",
      "severity": "must",
      "path": "pitfalls/monthly-means-are-time-averages.md",
      "sha256": "df6b5c23460f88f1522e5c6a665e1efbb8322c3ae876c885530f3019aa284ee0",
      "reason": "The explained variance and persistence are derived offline from monthly-mean `tos` fields, and this fragment warns that such diagnostics differ from those computed online or from higher-frequency data.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "Day weights do not enter a monthly climatology: each calendar month's climatology is the plain mean of that month's values over the 1950-2014 years, as the index tools build it."
